\title{Vertex isoperimetric problem}
\taxon{Definition}
\date{2023-10-01}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{Our main concern will be the discrete (rather than continuous) case. Given a graph #{G = (V, E)}, the \em{vertex isoperimetric problem} in #{G} is as follows: given a set #{A \subset V} of #{a} vertices, at least how large is its \em{closed neighbourhood}}

##{\begin{aligned}
N(A) &= \lbrace x \in V : d(x, A) \leq 1\rbrace  \\
&= A \cup \lbrace x \in V \setminus A : xy \in E \text{ for some vertex } y \in A\rbrace
\end{aligned}}

\p{in terms of #{a}?}

\p{In a variant of this problem, we start with a bipartite or #{r}-partite graph, and study the neighbourhood of a set #{A} contained in one of the parts. Thus, let #{G} be a bipartite graph with bipartition #{(V_1, V_2)} and edge set #{E}. Given a set #{A \subset V_1} with #{a} vertices, we should like to know at least how large the \em{open neighbourhood}}

##{\Gamma(A) = \lbrace y \in V_2 : xy \in E \text{ for some vertex } x \in A\rbrace}

\p{is in terms of #{a}. (Here it is nicer to take the open neighbourhood since the closed neighbourhood of a set #{A \subset V_1} is just #{N(A) = A \cup \Gamma(A)}.)}
